[38;5;252m[1m## One thing worth knowing[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mlong_exec[0m's nested loops are [38;5;10mf^200000[0m applied independently per element (2000 calls × 100 inner steps), which is 5.2×10¹⁰ kernel steps — the C [38;5;10m.so[0m takes about 8 minutes per seed. Since [38;5;10mf: i32 -> i32[0m is a self-map, every orbit is eventually periodic, so [38;5;10msrc/fast.rs[0m memoises the 8 cycles this map has (lengths 25…52330) and resolves each element with one modular index into its cycle. This is exact function-iteration algebra, not an approximation, and every heuristic in it falls back to plain iteration, so the heuristics affect only speed. Result: ~0.35 s per seed instead of ~8 min.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mperform_expensive_operations[0m itself is left as the literal 100-step loop, since it is a public symbol a caller may invoke directly. Unit tests assert the fast path equals naive iteration for 2000 random values across a dozen exponents and for [38;5;10mn[0m = 65536/199999/200000/200001.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One earlier attempt exceeded the 6 GB heap limit: for small [38;5;10mn[0m the walk terminates before reaching a cycle, so cycle discovery fired per element and allocated a fresh cycle vector each time. Fixed by gating discovery on [38;5;10mn[0m and de-duplicating basins by cycle position.[0m[0m
